WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.420
In 1991, architect Mick Pearce had a
problem.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.820
An investment group in Harare, Zimbabwe

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.520
hired him to design the largest
office and retail building in the country.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.960
But they didn't want to pay for
the expensive air conditioning

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.220
needed to cool such a large building.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.220
So that left
Pearce with a seemingly impossible challenge:

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.400
How do you design a building
that cools itself?

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.160
This is a termite mound.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.880
Millions of termites live inside these structures,

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.900
some of which stretch an astonishing 30
feet high.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.060
Although these termite skyscrapers may
look solid from the outside,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.340
they are actually covered in tiny holes that
allow air to pass through freely.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.340
Like a giant lung, the structure inhales and exhales as temperatures rise and fall throughout the day.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:15.020
This termite ventilation inspired Pearce to use an approach known as biomimicry,

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.400
imitating the ingenuity found in nature
to solve human problems.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.000
Meet the Eastgate Centre.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.200
The building is made from concrete slabs and brick.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.700
Just like the soil inside a termite mound,
 these materials have a high “thermal mass”—

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.240
which means they can absorb a lot of heat without really changing temperature.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.000
The exterior of the building is prickly like a cactus.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.880
By increasing the amount of surface area, 
heat loss is improved at night,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.120
while heat gain is reduced during the day.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.480
Inside the building, low-power fans 
pull in cool night air from outside

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.040
and disperse it throughout the seven floors.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.300
The concrete blocks absorb the cold, insulating 
the building and chilling the circulating air.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.180
When the morning comes and temperatures rise,
warm air is vented up through the ceiling

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.780
and released by the chimneys.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:23.760
Thanks to this innovative design, temperatures inside 
stay at a comfortable 82 degrees during the day

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.900
and 57 degrees at night.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.220
Not to mention, it uses up to 35 percent less energy
than similar buildings in Zimbabwe.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.480
Since opening its doors in 1996,

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.680
Mick Pearce's 90% natural climate control system

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.180
has made the Eastgate Centre a global landmark for sustainability.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.860
So, we must ask ourselves:

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.140
if an architect could design a self cooling building with termite inspired climate control,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.940
what other innovations can Mother Nature 
inspire if we just paid closer attention?

